=== TWO WORKSPACES — DO NOT CONFUSE ===

Your runtime has two repos that must NEVER be mixed up:

- **USER_REPO** (`{{ projects.0.path }}`) — the user's working checkout. They may have uncommitted changes, a non-main branch, WIP. **NEVER `git add`, `git commit`, `git checkout`, `git stash`, or `git pull` here.** The only sanctioned writes are via `protect-user-state.sh apply <patch>` + `protect-user-state.sh restore <id>` for UI-live-repro purposes — and `restore` ALWAYS runs even on error.
- **AGENT_WORKTREE** (`{{ install_dir }}/worktree/{{ projects.0.name }}`) — a `git worktree add`-created sibling on a detached HEAD. ALL agent git operations (checkout, diff, branch, push, etc.) happen here. The worktree is created/refreshed by `run.sh` before invoking you and pruned on exit.

When you generate a patch, you generate it AGAINST the agent worktree's tree. When you ship it as a PR, the PR's head branch is created in the agent worktree, then pushed. The user's working tree is read-only for inspection purposes only.

If you find yourself about to mutate USER_REPO outside the `protect-user-state.sh` flow — STOP. Re-target the operation to AGENT_WORKTREE.
{{#if projects.1}}

**Multi-project note:** This install has multiple configured projects. Each gets its own isolated worktree, exported as `NIGHT_SHIFT_AGENT_WORKTREE_0`, `NIGHT_SHIFT_AGENT_WORKTREE_1`, … (also `WORKTREE_<N>` shell vars inside `run.sh`).

Project → worktree map:
{{#each projects}}
- **{{ this.name }}** ({{ this.path }}) → `$NIGHT_SHIFT_AGENT_WORKTREE_{{ @index }}`{{#each this.verify_methods}}
  - verify[{{ this.name }}]: `{{ this.command }}`{{/each}}
{{/each}}

You may generate patches against **any** project. Per patch, set its `project_index` (the `N` above) so the wrapper knows which worktree it lives in.

**Snapshot/restore (multi-project):** `protect-user-state.sh snapshot` walks every USER_REPO_N — each project gets its own `snap-<id>/p<N>/` dir with HEAD, ref, status hash, and uncommitted diff. `rollback <snap-id>` iterates p<N>/ in numeric order. Primary failure aborts; secondary failure WARNs and continues (other projects still restore).

`begin <id> [--project=N]` and `apply <id> <patch> [--project=N]` accept a `--project=N` flag (default 0). Begins for the same `<id>` on different projects accumulate p<N>/ entries inside one checkpoint dir; `restore <id>` walks them all in numeric order. For a UI live-repro that spans two repos (e.g. mobile + backend), call `begin <id> --project=0` then `begin <id> --project=1`, apply the relevant test patch against whichever project actually needs to change with `apply <id> <patch> --project=N`, then one `restore <id>` rolls back both. Verify methods are project-specific — never run project A's `pytest` in project B's worktree.

`apply` takes an optional `base-ref` (3rd positional, default `origin/main`): `apply <id> <patch> <base-ref> [--project=N]`. **A patch generated against a PR / feature branch MUST pass that branch as base-ref** — resolve it with `gh pr view <N> --json headRefName --jq .headRefName` and pass `origin/<branch>`. Omitting it (or passing `origin/main`) silently fails for any patch that adds or touches files present only on the feature branch: `git apply`'s context won't match, the patch is skipped, and you get zero live verification with no error. Patches for bugs that already exist on prod (e.g. a bug-feed item) keep the `origin/main` default.

`WORKTREE` and `USER_REPO` (without index) alias the **primary** (project 0) for backward compat with single-project recipes. For multi-project recipes, always use the indexed form.
